--TEST--
`Core\Cli::write` and `echo` are one sink reached twice, on every shape that reaches it differently — ADR 0086 §§ 1 and 3
--FILE--
<?nvs
// § 1's substitution table has one home, and `write` performs that one rather
// than carrying a second copy. Asserted as *agreement* rather than as what
// either statement printed: the three shapes below reach the sink by three
// different routes — ordinary text, text carrying a control byte, and the one
// raw path — so a member that grew a table of its own fails here while still
// looking entirely plausible on any single line.
var $raw = Core\Str::fromCodePoint(27) . "[31mred";
var $carrier = Core\Cli\Text::plain($raw);

// Nothing to substitute: both write the bytes they were handed.
Core\Cli::write("plain text");
echo "\n";
echo "plain text", "\n";

// A control byte: both replace it with the visible glyph, because the rule is
// the sink's and not the statement's.
Core\Cli::write($raw);
echo "\n";
echo $raw, "\n";

// The carrier: both write it through unchanged, which is § 1's "exactly one raw
// path" read as a *type* — the class is what the sink recognises, so a second
// way of reaching the sink recognises it too.
Core\Cli::write($carrier);
echo "\n";
echo $carrier, "\n";
--EXPECT--
plain text
plain text
␛[31mred
␛[31mred
␛[31mred
␛[31mred
